                "img": "https://arxiv.org/html/2610.27954/2610.27954v1/concept_figure.png",
                "caption": "Figure 1:Comparison of error-handling strategies in computer-use agents. (A) Reflection proceeds from the erroneous state, (B) rollback requires often-infeasible environment resets, while (C) recovery restores a prior intact state and resumes the task.",
                "position": 334
            },
            {
                "img": "https://arxiv.org/html/2610.27954/2610.27954v1/main_figure.png",
                "caption": "Figure 2:Overview ofReTrace. (A) On-Policy Trajectory Rollout collects successful trajectories and replays each action sequenceNNtimes to build a transition-consistent dataset. (B) Trajectory Drift via Perturbation selects a recovery target and perturbs the agent into an erroneous state. (C) Goal-Oriented Action Replay replays the remaining actions from the recovered state to test whether the agent reproduces the original task outcome.",
                "position": 352
            }
        ]
